    "contextPassage": "READING PASSAGE 1\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 26–33, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below.\n\nTHE MYCORRHIZAL REVOLUTION: FUNGAL NETWORKS IN SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE\n\nFor over four hundred million years, land plants have maintained an intimate underground alliance with subterranean fungi. Known as mycorrhizae—derived from the Greek words for 'fungus' and 'root'—these symbiotic networks extend microscopically thin filaments called hyphae throughout the soil matrix. While early twentieth-century agronomists viewed soil primarily as an inert mechanical medium to be fertilized with synthetic nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, contemporary ecological science recognizes that up to ninety percent of terrestrial plant species depend intrinsically upon these fungal mutualisms for nutrient acquisition and disease resistance.\n\nThe physiological mechanism underpinning mycorrhizal symbiosis is an elegant bidirectional exchange. Plants channel up to thirty percent of their photosynthetic carbon—in the form of simple sugars and amino acids—downward into root tissues to nourish fungal colonies. In return, the extensive hyphal network, which can possess up to one hundred times greater surface area than plant root hairs alone, secretes specialized organic acids that dissolve tightly bound mineral phosphorus and micronutrients such as zinc and copper from mineral soil particles. These nutrients are then transported directly into the host plant's vascular tissue.\n\nFurthermore, recent research led by Dr. Suzanne Simard and global forestry biologists demonstrates that mycorrhizal networks serve as underground biological communication channels. When a host plant is attacked by herbivorous insects or fungal pathogens, chemical and electrical stress signals travel rapidly through interconnected hyphae to adjacent neighboring plants. In response, neighboring plants synthesize protective volatile compounds—such as jasmonic acid and defensive polyphenols—prior to experiencing physical infestation.\n\nDespite these evident benefits, intensive modern monoculture agriculture has severely degraded native mycorrhizal populations. Deep mechanized tillage physically ruptures fragile hyphal networks, while high concentrations of synthetic phosphate fertilizers suppress the biochemical signaling required for fungal colonization; when phosphorus is artificially abundant, host plants actively reject fungal colonization to conserve carbon. Transitioning to regenerative no-till methods and inoculating crops with bio-diverse fungal spores could reduce synthetic fertilizer demand by an estimated forty percent globally.",
